\label{pro:lbr} ({\it Left Brzezi\'{n}ski's crossed product}) (\cite{Br}): Let $A$ be an algebra, $H$ a vector space and $1_H\in H$. Let $G: H\otimes H\longrightarrow A\otimes H$ (write $G(x\otimes x')=x^G\otimes x'_G$ for all $x, x'\in H$) and $R: H\otimes A\longrightarrow A\otimes H$ (write $R(x\otimes a)=a_R\otimes x_R$ for all $x\in H$ and $a\in A$) be linear maps such that
 $$
 (LB1)~~a_R\otimes1_{H R}=a\otimes {1_H},~~~{1_A}_R\otimes x_R={1_A}\otimes x
 $$
 holds. Then $A\#^G_R H$ ($=A\otimes H$ as vector space) is an algebra with unit $1_A\otimes 1_H$ and a product
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:lbr}
 (a\otimes x)(a'\otimes x')=a a'_R{x_R}^G\otimes x'_G
 \end{eqnarray}
 if and only if the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(LB2)& (a a')_R\otimes x_R=a_R a'_r\otimes x_{R r};\\
 &(LB3)& x^G\otimes {1_H}_G={1_H}^G\otimes x_G=1_A\otimes x;\\
 &(LB4)& {x'^G}_R{x_R}^g\otimes x''_{G g}=x^G{x'_G}^g\otimes x''_g;\\
 &(LB5)& a_{R r}{x_r}^G\otimes x'_{R G}=x^G a_R\otimes x'_{G R}
 \end{eqnarray*}
 for all $a, a'\in A$, $x, x', x''\in H$, where $G=g$, $R=r$.

The sufficiency can be gotten by \cite{Br}. So we only prove the necessity.
 By $1_A\otimes 1_H$ is unit and Eqs.$(LB1)$ and (\ref{eq:lbr}), we can get Eq.$(LB3)$. By the associativity and Eq.(\ref{eq:lbr}), we have
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:lbr-1}
 a{a'_R}{x_R}^G{a''_r}{x'_{Gr}}^g\otimes x''_g=a(a'a''_R{x'_R}^G)_r{x_r}^g\otimes x''_{Gg}.
 \end{eqnarray}
 Set $x'=x''=1_H$ in Eq.(\ref{eq:lbr-1}), one can obtain Eq.$(LB2)$. Let $a'=a''=1_A$ in Eq.(\ref{eq:lbr-1}), then Eq.$(LB4)$ holds. Likewise, setting $a'=1_A, x''=1_H$ in Eq.(\ref{eq:lbr-1}), we can get Eq.$(LB5)$. These finish the proof.

\label{ex:1.3} ({\it Left crossed product}) (\cite{BCM}): Let $H$ be a Hopf algebra with a left weak action $\triangleright$ on the algebra $A$. Let $\sigma: H\otimes H\longrightarrow A$ be a $K$-linear map. Let $A\#^{\sigma}_{\triangleright} H$ be the (usually nonassociative) algebra whose underlying space is $A\otimes H$ and whose multiplication is given by
 $$
 (a\otimes x)(a'\otimes x')=a(x_1\triangleright a')\sigma(x_2, x'_1)\otimes x_3x'_2
 $$
 for all $a, a'\in A$ and $x, x'\in H$. This $A\#^{\sigma}_{\triangleright} H$ is an associative algebra with unit $1_A\otimes {1_H}$ if and only if $\sigma$ satisfies Eq.(LTC3) and the following conditions:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(LC2)& (x_1\triangleright \sigma(x'_1, x''_1))\sigma(x_2, x'_2x''_2)=\sigma(x_1,x'_1)\sigma(x_2x'_2, x'');\\
 &(LC3)& (x_1\triangleright (x'_1\triangleright a))\sigma(x_2, x'_2)=\sigma(x_1,x'_1)(x_2x'_2\triangleright a)
 \end{eqnarray*}
 for all $a\in A$ and $x, x', x''\in H$.

\label{ex:1.4} ({\it Left twisted product}) (\cite{BCM}): Let $H$ be a bialgebra and $A$ an algebra. Let $\sigma: H\otimes H\longrightarrow A$ be a $K$-linear map. Let $A\#^{\sigma} H$ (=$A\otimes H$ as a vector space)  is an associative algebra with unit $1_A\otimes {1_H}$ and multiplication
 $$
 (a\otimes x)(a'\otimes x')=a a'\sigma(x_1, x'_1)\otimes x_2 x'_2
 $$
 for all $a, a'\in A$ and $x, x'\in H$ if and only if $\sigma$ satisfies Eq.(LTC3) and the following conditions:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(L2)& \sigma(x''_1, x'_1)\sigma(x, x''_2x'_2)=\sigma(x_1,x''_1)\sigma(x_2x''_2, x');\\
 &(L3)&  a \sigma(x, x')=\sigma(x, x') a
 \end{eqnarray*}
 for all $a\in A$ and $x, x', x''\in H$.

\label{ex:1.6} ({\it Left twisted tensor product}) (\cite{CIMZ}):  Let $H$, $A$ be two algebras and $R: H\otimes A\longrightarrow A\otimes H$ a linear map. Then $A\#_R H$ (=$A\otimes H$ as a vector space)  is an associative algebra with unit $1_A\otimes {1_H}$ and multiplication
 $$
 (a\otimes x)(a'\otimes x')=a{a'_R} \otimes x_R x'
 $$
 for all $a, a'\in A$ and $x, x'\in H$ if and only if  Eqs.(LB1), (LB2) and the following condition hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(LT3)& {a_{R}}\otimes (x x')_{R}={a}_{R r}\otimes x_{r}{x'_{R}},
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $a, a'\in A$, $x, x'\in H$ and $r=R$.

\label{de:1.9}({\it Majid's double biproduct}) We recall, from \cite{Ma99,Ma97}, the construction of the so-called double biproduct. Let $H$ be a bialgebra, $A$ a bialgebra in ${}^H_H{\mathbb{YD}}$, and $B$ a bialgebra in ${\mathbb{YD}}{}^H_H$. Adopt the following notation for the structure maps: the counits are
 $\varepsilon_A$ and $\varepsilon_B$, the comultiplications are $\Delta_A(a)=a_1\otimes a_2$ and $\Delta_B(b)=b_1\otimes b_2$, and the actions and coactions are
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &&H\otimes A\longrightarrow A,  x\otimes~~~a\mapsto  x\triangleright a,~~~A\longrightarrow H\otimes A,~~~a \mapsto  a_{-1}\otimes a_0,\\
 &&B\otimes H\longrightarrow B,~~~b\otimes x\mapsto b\triangleleft x,~~~~B\longrightarrow  B\otimes H,~~~b\mapsto  b_{[0]}\otimes  b_{[1]}
 \end{eqnarray*}
 for all $x\in  H$, $a\in  A$, $b\in B$. The vector space $A\otimes H\otimes B$ becomes an algebra (called the two-sided smash product, $A\# H\# B$) with unit $1_A\otimes 1_H\otimes 1_B$ and multiplication
 \begin{eqnarray}
 (a\otimes x\otimes b)(a' \otimes x' \otimes b') = a(x_1\triangleright a')\otimes x_2x'_1\otimes (b\triangleleft  x'_2)b', \label{eq:tssp}
 \end{eqnarray}
 and a coalgebra (called the two-sided smash coproduct, $A\times H\times B$) with
 counit $\varepsilon(a\otimes x\otimes b)=\varepsilon_A(a)\varepsilon_H(x)\varepsilon_B(b)$ and comultiplication
 \begin{eqnarray}
 &&\Delta(a\otimes x\otimes b)=a_1\otimes a_{2-1}x_1\otimes b_{1{[0]}} \otimes a_{20}\otimes x_2b_{1{[1]}}\otimes b_2. \label{eq:tssc}
 \end{eqnarray}
 Moreover, assume that Eq.(\ref{eq:DB}) holds, it follows that $A\otimes H\otimes B$ with two-sided smash product algebra and two-sided smash product coalgebra is a bialgebra, called the Majid's double biproduct, denoted by $A\star H\star B$.

\label{pro:2.1}({\it Right Brzezi\'{n}ski's crossed product}) (\cite[Theorem 2.1]{Pa14}) Let $H$ be a vector space with a distinguished element $1_H\in H$ and $B$ an algebra. Let $F: H\otimes H\longrightarrow H\otimes B$ (write $F(x\otimes x')=x_F\otimes x'^F$ for all $x, x'\in H$) and $T: B\otimes H\longrightarrow H\otimes B$ (write $T(b\otimes x)=x_T\otimes b_T$ for all $x\in H$ and $b\in B$) be two linear maps such that
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RB1)& 1_{H T}\otimes b_T=1_H\otimes b,~x_T\otimes 1_{B T}=x\otimes 1_B
 \end{eqnarray*}
 holds. Then $H^F_T\# B$ (=$H\otimes B$ as a vector space) is an associative algebra with unit $1_H\otimes {1_B}$ and multiplication
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 (x\otimes b)(x'\otimes b')=x_F\otimes {x'_T}^F b_T b'
 \end{eqnarray*}
 for all $b, b'\in B$ and $x, x'\in H$ if and only if the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RB2)& x_{T}\otimes (b b')_{T}=x_{T t}\otimes b_{t} b'_{T};\\
 &(RB3)& 1_{H F}\otimes x^{F}=x_{F}\otimes {1_H}^{F}=x\otimes 1_{B};\\
 &(RB4)& x_{F f}\otimes {x''_{T}}^{f}{x'^{F}}_{T}=x_{f}\otimes {x'_{F}}^{f}x''^{F};\\
 &(RB5)& x_{T F}\otimes {x'_{t}}^{F}{b}_{Tt}=x_{F T}\otimes {b_{T}}x'^{F},
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $b, b'\in B$, $x, x', x''\in H$ and $f=F, t=T$.

\label{ex:2.2} (1) {\it (Right twisted crossed product)} Let $B$ be an algebra, $H$ a bialgebra, $\tau: H\otimes H\longrightarrow B$ and $T: B\otimes H\longrightarrow H\otimes B$ two linear maps such that  Eq.(RB1) holds. Then $H^{\tau}_T\#  B$ (=$H\otimes B$ as a vector space)  is an associative algebra with unit $1_H\otimes {1_B}$ and multiplication
 $$
 (x\otimes b)(x'\otimes b')=x_1 x'_{T1} \otimes \tau(x_2, x'_{T2}) b_T b'
 $$
 for all $a, a'\in A$ and $x, x'\in H$ if and only if  Eq.(RB2) and the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RTC2)& \tau(x, 1_H)=\tau(1_H, x)=\varepsilon(x)1_B;\\
 &(RTC3)& x_1 x'_1 x''_{T1}\otimes \tau(x_2 x'_2, x''_{T2})\tau(x_3, x'_3)_{T}=x_1 x'_1 x''_{1}\otimes \tau(x_2,  x'_2x''_{2})\tau(x'_3, x''_3);\\
 &(RTC4)& x_{T1}x'_{t1}\otimes \tau(x_{T2}, x'_{t2}){b}_{Tt}=(x_1x'_1)_{T}\otimes {b_{T}}\tau(x_2, x'_2),
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $b, b'\in B$, $x, x', x''\in H$ and $t=T$.

 \begin{proof} Let $F(x\otimes x')=x_1 x'_1\otimes \tau (x_2, x'_2)$ in Proposition \ref{pro:2.1}.      \end{proof}

 (2) {\it (Right unified product)} Let $B$, $H$ be two bialgebras and $\blacktriangleright: B\otimes H\longrightarrow H$, $\blacktriangleleft: B\otimes H\longrightarrow B$, $\tau: H\otimes H\longrightarrow B$ linear maps such that
 $$
 (RU1)~~~~b_1\blacktriangleright {1_H}\otimes b_2\blacktriangleleft {1_H}={1_H}\otimes b;~~1_B\blacktriangleright x_1\otimes 1_B\blacktriangleleft x_2=x\otimes 1_B
 $$
 holds. Then $H^{\tau}_{\blacktriangleleft \blacktriangleright}\# B$ (=$H\otimes B$ as a vector space)  is an associative algebra with unit ${1_H}\otimes 1_B$ and multiplication
 $$
 (x\otimes b)(x'\otimes b')=x_1(b_1\blacktriangleright x'_1)_1 \otimes \tau(x_2, (b_1\blacktriangleright x'_1)_2)(b_2\blacktriangleleft x'_2)b'
 $$
 for all $x, x'\in H$ and $b, b'\in B$ if and only if the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RU2)& b_1b'_1\blacktriangleright x_1\otimes b_2b'_2\blacktriangleleft x_2=b_1\blacktriangleright (b'_1\blacktriangleright x_1)_1\otimes (b_2\blacktriangleleft (b'_1\blacktriangleright x_1)_2)(b'_2\blacktriangleleft x_2);\\
 &(RU3)& x_1\otimes \tau(1_H, x_2)=x_1\otimes \tau(x_2, 1_H)=x\otimes 1_B;\\
 &(RU4)& ~~x_1x'_1(\tau(x_3, x'_3)_1\blacktriangleright x''_1)_1\otimes \tau(x_2x'_2, (\tau(x_3, x'_3)_1\blacktriangleright x''_1)_2)(\tau(x_3, x'_3)_2\blacktriangleright x''_2)\\
 & &=x_1x'_1x''_1\otimes \tau(x_2, x'_2x''_2)\tau(x'_3, x''_3);\\
 &(RU5)&(b_1\blacktriangleright x_1)_1((b_2\blacktriangleleft x_2)_1\blacktriangleright x'_1)_1\otimes \tau((b_1\blacktriangleright x_1)_2, ((b_2\blacktriangleleft x_2)_1\blacktriangleright x'_1)_2) \\
 &&\times ((b_2\blacktriangleleft x_2)_2\blacktriangleleft x'_2)=b_1\blacktriangleright x_1x'_1\otimes (b_2\blacktriangleleft x_2x'_2)\tau(x_3, x'_3),
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $b, b'\in B$, $x, x', x''\in H$.

 \begin{proof}  Let $T(b\otimes x)=b_1\blacktriangleright x_1 \otimes b_2\blacktriangleleft x_2$ in $H^{\tau}_T\#  B$.      \end{proof}

 (3) {\it (Right $F$-twist product)} Let $H$ be a vector space with a distinguished element $1_H\in H$ and $B$ an algebra. Let $F: H\otimes H\longrightarrow A\otimes H$ (write $F(x\otimes x')=x_F\otimes x'^F$ for all $x, x'\in H$) be a linear maps. Then $H^F\# B$ (=$H\otimes B$ as a vector space)  is an associative algebra with unit $1_H\otimes {1_B}$ and multiplication
 $$
 (x\otimes b)(x'\otimes b')=x_F\otimes {x'}^F b b'
 $$
 for all $b, b'\in B$ and $x, x'\in H$ if and only if  Eq.(RB3) and the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RF2)& x_{F f}\otimes {x''}^{f}{x'^{F}}=x_{f}\otimes {x'_{F}}^{f}x''^{F};\\
 &(RF3)& x_{F}\otimes {x'}^{F}{b}=x_{F}\otimes {b}x'^{F},
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $b, b'\in B$, $x, x', x''\in H$ and $f=F$.

 \begin{proof} Let $T$ be trivial in Proposition \ref{pro:2.1}.    \end{proof}

 Here we remark that if we set $F(x, x')=x_1 x'_1\otimes \tau (x_2, x'_2)$ in $H^F\# B$, then we get the {\it right twist product} $H^{\tau}\# B$ and the conditions $(RB3), (RF2)$, $(RF3)$ become the conditions $(RTC2)$ and
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(R2)& \tau(x_1x'_1, x'')\tau(x_2, x'_2)=\tau(x, x'_1x''_1)\tau(x'_2, x''_2);\\
 &(R3)& \tau(x, x')b=b\tau(x, x'),
 \end{eqnarray*}
 which is the right version of $(LTC3), (L2)$, $(L3)$.

 (4) {\it(Right twisted tensor product)} Let $H$, $B$ be two algebras and $T: B\otimes H\longrightarrow H\otimes B$ (write $T(b\otimes x)=x_T\otimes b_T$ for all $x\in H$ and $b\in B$) a linear map. Then $H_T\# B$ (=$H\otimes B$ as a vector space)  is an associative algebra with unit $1_H\otimes {1_B}$ and multiplication
 $$
 (x\otimes b)(x'\otimes b')=x{x'_T} \otimes b_T b'
 $$
 for all $b, b'\in B$ and $x, x'\in H$ if and only if  Eqs.(RB1), (RB2) and the following condition hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RT3)& x_{T}{x'_{t}}\otimes {b}_{Tt}=(x x')_{T}\otimes {b_{T}},
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $b, b'\in B$, $x, x'\in H$ and $t=T$.

 \begin{proof}  Let $F$ be trivial, i.e., $F(x\otimes x')=x x'\otimes 1_B$ in Proposition \ref{pro:2.1}.             \end{proof}



 (5) {\it (Right crossed product)} (\cite{MLL}) Let $H$ be a Hopf algebra with a right weak action $\triangleleft$ on the algebra $B$. Let $\tau: H\otimes H\longrightarrow B$ be a $K$-linear map. Let $H^{\tau}_{\triangleleft}\# B$ be the (usually nonassociative) algebra whose underlying space is $H\otimes B$ and whose multiplication is given by
 $$
(x\otimes b)(x'\otimes b')=x_1x'_1\otimes \tau(x_2, x'_2)(a\triangleleft x'_3)b'
 $$
 for all $b, b'\in B$ and $x, x'\in H$. This $H^{\tau}_{\triangleleft}\# B$ is an associative algebra with unit $1_H\otimes {1_B}$ if and only if $\tau$ satisfies Eq.(RTC2) and the following conditions:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(RC2)&\tau(x_1x'_1, x''_1)(\tau(x_2, x'_2)\triangleleft x''_2)=\tau(x, x'_1x''_1)\tau(x'_2, x''_2);\\
 &(RC3)&\tau(x'_1, x''_1)((b\triangleleft x'_2)\triangleleft x''_2)=(b\triangleleft x'_1x''_1)\tau(x'_2, x''_2)
 \end{eqnarray*}
 for all $b\in B$ and $x, x', x''\in H$.

 \begin{proof}  Let $T(b\otimes x)=x_1\otimes b\triangleleft x_2$ in $H^{\tau}_T\#  B$.                                    \end{proof}

We only prove the necessity of $(BT4)$ as follows.  And the rest is straightforward by letting $\sigma$ and $\tau$ be trivial in $A\#{_{R}^{\ \sigma} H^{\tau}_{T}}\# B$.
 By  the associativity in $A\#{_{R} H_{T}}\# B$, we can get
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 a a'_{R}a''_{r}\otimes (x_{R} x'_{T})_ r x''_{t}\otimes (b_{T}b')_{t}b''=a (a'a''_{R})_{r}\otimes x_{r}(x'_{R}x''_{T})_t\otimes b_{t}b'_{T}b''.
 \end{eqnarray*}
 Let $a=a'=1_A$, $b'=b''=1_B$ and $x'=x''=1_H$ in the above equation. Then we obtain $(BT4)$.

\label{de:3.2} Let $A, B$ be two algebras, and $H$ be a coalgebra with a distinguished element $1_H\in H$. Let $G:H\otimes H\longrightarrow A\otimes H$ , $\tau:H\otimes H\longrightarrow B$ and $ S:H\longrightarrow H$ be linear maps. Then $S$ is called a {\it $(G,\tau)$-antipode of $H$} if for all $x\in H$, the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 (I1)~~{S(x_1)_1}^{G}\otimes x_{2 G}\otimes \tau(S(x_1)_2,x_3)=1_A\otimes 1_H\otimes 1_B\varepsilon_H(x);\\
 (I2)~~{x_1}^{G}\otimes S(x_3)_{1G}\otimes \tau(x_2,S(x_3)_2)=1_A\otimes 1_H\otimes 1_B\varepsilon_H(x).
 \end{eqnarray*}
 In this case, we call $H$ a $(G,\tau)$-Hopf algebra.

\label{pro:3.3}  Let $A, B$ be two Hopf algebras with antipode $S_A, S_B$, $H$ a coalgebra with an element $1_H$ such that $\Delta(1_H)=1_H\otimes 1_H$ and $S_H: H\longrightarrow H$ a linear map. Suppose that $A\star {_{R}^{G} H^{\tau}_{T}}\star B$ is a Brzezi\'{n}ski's two-sided crossed product bialgebra. Then $A\star {_{R}^{G} H^{\tau}_{T}}\star B$ is a Hopf algebra with antipode $\overline{S}$ defined by
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:s}
 \overline{S}(a\otimes x\otimes b)=(1_A\otimes 1_H\otimes S_B(b))(1_A\otimes S_H(x)\otimes 1_B)(S_A(a)\otimes 1_H\otimes 1_B)
 \end{eqnarray}
 if and only if $H$ is a $(G,\tau)$-Hopf algebra.

\label{cor:3.1b} Let $H$ be a bialgebra. Let $\sigma: H\otimes H\rightarrow A$ be a linear map, where $A$ is a bialgebra with a left $H$-weak action such that $\varepsilon_A(\sigma(x, x'))=\varepsilon_H(x)\varepsilon_H(x')$. Let $\tau: H\otimes H\rightarrow B$ be a linear map, where $B$ is a bialgebra with a right $H$-weak action such that $\varepsilon_B(1_B)=1, \varepsilon_B(\tau(x,x'))=\varepsilon_H(x)\varepsilon_H(x')$. The two-sided crossed product $A\#^{\sigma} H~^{\tau}\# B$ equipped with the two-sided tensor product coalgebra $A\otimes H\otimes B$ becomes a bialgebra if and only if Eqs.$(J2)$-$(J6)$, $(D4)$, $(F3)$, $(F4)$  and the following condition holds:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(P1) & x_{2} \otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a)=x_{1} \otimes (x_{2}\triangleright a),~~~ (b\triangleleft x_{2}) \otimes x_{1} =(b \triangleleft x_{1})\otimes x_{2},
 \end{eqnarray*}
 where $x\in H, a\in A$. In this case, we call this bialgebra {\it two-sided crossed product bialgebra} and denoted by $A\star {^{\sigma} H^{\tau}}\star B$. Furthermore, $A\star {^{\sigma} H^{\tau}}\star B$ is a Hopf algebra with antipode Eq.(\ref{eq:s}) if and only if $H$ is a $(\sigma,\tau)$-Hopf algebra.

\label{ex:ss}
 Let $H=KC_2=K\{1, a\}$ and $A=B=KC_4=K\{1, x, x^2, x^3\}$ be group Hopf algebras (\cite{Ra12}), where $C_i$ is cyclic group with order $i$, $i=2,4$. Define the linear maps $\triangleright: H\otimes A\longrightarrow A$, $\triangleleft: B\otimes H\longrightarrow B$, $\sigma: H\otimes H\longrightarrow A$ and $\tau:H\otimes H\longrightarrow B$ by
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &&1_H\triangleright 1_A=1_A,~1_H\triangleright x=x,~1_H\triangleright x^2=x^2,~1_H\triangleright x^3=x^3,\\
 &&a\triangleright 1_A=1_A,~a\triangleright x=x^3,~a\triangleright x^2=x^2,~a\triangleright x^3=x;
 \end{eqnarray*}
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &&1_B\triangleleft 1_H=1_B,~x\triangleleft 1_H=x,~x^2\triangleleft 1_H=x^2,~x^3\triangleleft 1_H=x^3,\\
 &&1_B\triangleleft a=1_B,~x\triangleleft a=x^3,~ x^2\triangleleft a=x^2,~ x^3\triangleleft a=x;
 \end{eqnarray*}
 and
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &&\sigma(1_H, 1_H)=1_A,~\sigma(1_H, a)=1_A,~\sigma(a, 1_H)=1_A,~\sigma(a, a)=x;\\
 &&\tau(1_H, 1_H)=1_A,~\tau(1_H, a)=1_A,~\tau(a, 1_H)=1_A,~\tau(a, a)=x,
 \end{eqnarray*}
 then by \cite[Example 2.11]{AM08}, we know that $\triangleright$ is a weak action of $H$ on $A$ and $\triangleleft$ is a weak action of $H$ on $B$. Thus we can get two-sided crossed product $A\# {^{\sigma} H^{\tau}}\# B$. Also by Corollary \ref{cor:3.1b}, two-sided crossed product $A\#^{\sigma} H~^{\tau}\# B$ equipped with the two-sided tensor product coalgebra $A\otimes H\otimes B$ becomes a bialgebra, since $H$ and $A, B$ are cocommucative. But unfortunately $\bar{S}$ defined by Eq.(\ref{eq:s}) is not an antipode of $A\star {^{\sigma} H^{\tau}}\star B$, because Eq.(I1) doesn't hold for $a\in H$.

\label{pro:dcb-1} Let $H$ be a bialgebra. Let $\sigma: H\otimes H\rightarrow A$ be a linear map, where $A$ is a left $H$-comodule coalgebra such that $\varepsilon_A(1_A)=1$, and an algebra with a left $H$-weak action. Let $B$ be a right $H$-module algebra and also a comodule coalgebra such that $\varepsilon_B(1_B)=1$. The one-sided crossed product $A\#^{\sigma} H\# B$ equipped with the two-sided smash coproduct $A\times H\times B$ becomes a bialgebra if and only if Eqs.$(C1)$-$(C5)$, $(D7)$, $(D8)$, $(D10)$-$(D12)$, $(D15)$-$(D17)$, $(D20)$, $(D21)$ and the following conditions hold:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &(E1) &(bb')_{1}\otimes (bb')_{2}=b_{1}b'_{1[0]}\otimes (b_{2}\triangleleft b'_{1[1]})b'_{2};\\
 &(E2) &(b\triangleleft x)_{1}\otimes (b\triangleleft x)_{2}=(b_{1}\triangleleft x_{1})\otimes (b_{2}\triangleleft x_{2});\\
 &(E3) &(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{2})b'\otimes  (x_{1}\triangleright a)\sigma (x_{2},x'_{1})=(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a)\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]},x'_{2}b'_{[1]}) .
 \end{eqnarray*}

\label{rmk:dcb-1}
 (1) By Eq.$(D21)$, Eq.(\ref{eq:DB}) in the Majid's double biproduct is not only sufficient but also necessary. \newline
 \indent{\phantom{\bf Remarks}}\quad (2) The conditions in Proposition \ref{pro:dcb-1} are equivalent to the ones in \cite[Theorem 3.2]{MJS}.

 \begin{proof}  By Eq.$(D22)$, we have
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:d22-1}
 (b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes a(x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]},x'_{2}b'_{[1]})=(b\triangleleft x'_{2})b'\otimes a(x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2},x'_{1}).
  \end{eqnarray}
 Setting $a=1=a', b'=1$ in Eq.(\ref{eq:d22-1}), we have
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:d22-2}
 (b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})\otimes \sigma (xb_{[1]},x'_{2})=(b\triangleleft x'_{2})\otimes \sigma(x,x'_{1}).
 \end{eqnarray}
 Setting $a=1=a', b=1$ in Eq.(\ref{eq:d22-1}), we get
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:d22-3}
 b'_{[0]}\otimes \sigma (x,x'b'_{[1]})=b'\otimes \sigma(x,x').
 \end{eqnarray}
 On the other hand, we have
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 &&(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes a(x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]},x'_{2}b'_{[1]})\\
 &\stackrel{(\ref{eq:d22-3})}{=} &(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'\otimes a(x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]},x'_{2})\\
 &\stackrel{(\ref{eq:d22-2})}{=} &(b\triangleleft x'_{2})b'\otimes a(x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2},x'_{1}).
  \end{eqnarray*}
 Therefore Eq.(\ref{eq:d22-1})$\Leftrightarrow$ Eqs.(\ref{eq:d22-2}) and (\ref{eq:d22-3}).

 $(\Longrightarrow)$ We only check that Eq.$(C14)$ in \cite[Theorem 3.2]{MJS} holds. In fact, one can compute as follows.
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 \hbox{LHS~ of~Eq}.(C14)
 &\stackrel{ }{=}&(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{2})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2},x'_{1})\otimes x_3b_{[1]}x'_3b'_{[1]}\\
 &\stackrel{(\ref{eq:d22-2})}{=} &(b_{[0][0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2}b_{[0][1]},x'_{2})\otimes x_3b_{[1]}x'_3b'_{[1]}\\
 &\stackrel{(\ref{eq:d22-3})}{=} &(b_{[0][0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0][0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2}b_{[0][1]},x'_{2}b'_{[0][1]})\otimes  x_3b_{[1]}x'_3b'_{[1]}\\
 &\stackrel{ }{=}&(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]1},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\otimes   x_3b_{[1]2}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &\stackrel{(\ref{eq:d20-1})}{=} &(b_{[0][0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2},b_{[0][1]})\sigma(x_{3}b_{[1]1},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\otimes x_4b_{[1]2}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &\stackrel{ }{=} &(b_{[0]}\triangleleft x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2},b_{[1]1})\sigma(x_{3}b_{[1]2},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\otimes x_4b_{[1]3}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &\stackrel{(D21)}{=} &(b_{[0][0]}\triangleleft a'_{-1}x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes [x_{1}\triangleright (b_{[0][1]}\triangleright a'_{0})]\sigma(x_{2},b_{[1]1})\sigma(x_{3}b_{[1]2},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\\
 &&\otimes x_4b_{[1]3}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &\stackrel{ }{=} &(b_{[0]}\triangleleft a'_{-1}x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes [x_{1}\triangleright (b_{[1]1}\triangleright a'_{0})]\sigma(x_{2},b_{[1]2})\sigma(x_{3}b_{[1]3},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\\
 &&\otimes x_4b_{[1]4}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &\stackrel{(LC3)}{=} &(b_{[0]}\triangleleft a'_{-1}x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes \sigma(x_{1},b_{[1]1})(x_{2}b_{[1]2}\triangleright a'_{0})\sigma(x_{3}b_{[1]3},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\\
 &&\otimes x_4b_{[1]4}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &\stackrel{(D20)}{=} &(b_{[0]}\triangleleft a'_{-1}x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}b_{[1]1}\triangleright a'_{0})\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]2},x'_{2}b'_{[1]1})\otimes x_3b_{[1]3}x'_3b'_{[1]2}\\
 &=&\hbox{RHS~ of~Eq}.(C14)
 \end{eqnarray*}

 $(\Longleftarrow)$ Let $b=1, a'=1, x'=1$ in Eq.$(C14)$, then we can get Eq.$(D20)$; Applying $id\otimes id\otimes \varepsilon$ to Eq.$(C14)$, one can obtain
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:c14-1}
 (b\triangleleft x'_{2})b'\otimes (x_{1}\triangleright a')\sigma(x_{2},x'_{1})=(b_{[0]}\triangleleft a'_{-1}x'_{1})b'_{[0]}\otimes (x_{1}b_{[1]1}\triangleright a'_{0})\sigma(x_{2}b_{[1]2},x'_{2}b'_{[1]}).
 \end{eqnarray}
 Let $a'=1, b'=1$ in Eq.(\ref{eq:c14-1}), we get Eq.(\ref{eq:d22-2}). Likewise, we can obtain Eqs.(\ref{eq:d22-3}) and $(D21)$.

 Up to now, we have checked that Eqs.$(D20), (D21), (E3)$ $\Leftrightarrow$ Eq.$(C14)$ in \cite[Theorem 3.2]{MJS}. And the rest is obvious.  \end{proof}

